# SRG-APP-000296-AU-000560  -  CAT II
# The Central Log Server must provide a logout capability for user initiated
# communication session.
#
# Generated from the benchmark and the assessment determinations, then kept
# by hand. Edit this file to change how this requirement is assessed here;
# it is read at run time. Regenerating refreshes the benchmark text in place
# and leaves the criteria, the determination and any verification block as
# they are.

id:              SRG-APP-000296-AU-000560
group:           V-221923
severity:        medium
cat:             CAT II
determination:   APPLICABLE
grade:           C
decidable:       manual
confidence:      derived
revised:         yes
cci:             CCI-002363
control_rev5:    AC-12

contribution:    Splunk Web provides a logout control in its own interface for every
                 authenticated session and no configuration setting removes it, so the
                 application already carries this requirement as far as the product allows.

external:        Where authentication is delegated to a SAML identity provider, the
                 identity provider must publish and honor a single-logout endpoint at the
                 configured idpSLOUrl and binding; Splunk can send the logout request but
                 cannot make the identity provider end its own session.
external:        Invalidating session tokens cluster-wide on logout requires search head
                 clustering and the App Key Value store to be enabled, which is deployment
                 architecture rather than a value assertable on a single instance.
external:        Closing the browser or the remote-access session that carries it is the
                 user's action and the hosting platform's, not the application's.

manual_reason:   No setting in the 10.4.2 specification set enables, disables or weakens
                 the logout control; Splunk Web presents it unconditionally, so an assessor
                 confirms the capability in the interface rather than reading a value.

citation:        Each of the five reviewed conditions produces a false finding. server.conf
                 [general] invalidateSessionTokensOnLogout "has an effect only if search
                 head clustering and App Key Value store are enabled" and defaults to false
                 (server.conf.spec:81-87), so requiring it true fails a standalone indexer
                 or search head where the setting does nothing at all.
                 logoutCacheRefreshInterval "has no effect when
                 'invalidateSessionTokensOnLogout' is disabled" and defaults to 30s
                 (server.conf.spec:89-92), so requiring it present tests nothing and can
                 only fail an instance that has no reason to declare it. The three
                 authentication.conf conditions sit in a [<saml-authSettings-key>] stanza
                 that exists only where authType is SAML, against a default authType of
                 Splunk (authentication.conf.spec:30-33, 44), and because conditions are
                 ANDed they report a finding on every instance not using SAML; idpSLOUrl
                 and redirectAfterLogoutToUrl have no defaults
                 (authentication.conf.spec:687-690, 780-784), and sloBinding's "Acceptable
                 options are 'HTTPPost' and 'HTTPRedirect'" with HTTPPost the default
                 (authentication.conf.spec:1109-1113) means in:HTTPPost|HTTPRedirect cannot
                 fail any legal value and decides nothing. Since no setting governs whether
                 the logout capability exists, this is case 4: there is nothing to test,
                 and the single-logout and cluster-token behavior belongs in external
                 dependencies where it is stated rather than silently asserted.
finding:         Not expected to be a finding against the application: Splunk Web presents
                 a logout control for user-initiated sessions unconditionally, and an
                 assessor records that observation; raise a finding only if a logout
                 observed in the interface leaves the session usable.
